<Article slug="redis-cluster">
<p>Our application caches a product card and its stock summary. On one Redis instance, an MGET can retrieve both keys. We divide the cache across three primaries, keep the same key names, and try again. Redis replies <code>CROSSSLOT</code>.</p>
<p>The two answers still exist. The operation no longer fits the way the keyspace is divided. Before adding more Redis processes, we need to understand what is being divided—and which operations must remain together.</p>
<p><strong>Redis Cluster</strong> partitions the keyspace across primaries. Each primary can have replicas, giving us both distribution across shards and copies within a shard. A replica adds another copy of its primary’s portion; it does not give every primary a copy of the whole cache.</p>

<h2>Hash slots and hash tags</h2>
<p>Cluster routes through 16,384 <strong>hash slots</strong>. It calculates a slot from the key’s bytes, then looks up the primary that currently owns that slot. The number of primaries is not part of the key’s slot calculation. Adding a primary can change who owns a slot without changing which keys belong to it.</p>
<p>Try the two ordinary key names below, then use the shared hash tag.</p>
<ClusterRouting />
<p>The native Redis 8.2.2 lab reports slot 9,762 for <code>card:42</code> and 2,594 for <code>stock:42</code>. In our assigned ranges, they belong to B and A respectively. One ordinary MGET across those keys is rejected.</p>
<p>The names <code>card:&#123;42&#125;:a</code> and <code>card:&#123;42&#125;:b</code> share a <strong>hash tag</strong>: Redis hashes the non-empty substring inside the first matching brace pair. Both produce slot 8,000. Their values can now participate in one same-slot MGET, which the lab returns as <code>[12, stock]</code>.</p>
<p>The precise boundary is the slot, not the current machine. Two different slots can happen to have the same owner today and move apart tomorrow. Ordinary multi-key commands, transactions and scripts should be designed around the same-slot boundary. A client may offer a convenience operation that scatters independent requests across nodes, but that is not one atomic server operation.</p>
<p>Choose a tag for the smallest group that must be operated on together. Tagging every key with the same tenant ID makes tenant-wide grouping easy, but also concentrates that tenant’s keys. A large tenant can become a large shard workload. If only one product’s related entries need atomic treatment, a product-scoped tag may be a better boundary.</p>
<p>Co-location does not replace the <a href="/learn/databases/caching/keys-and-values/">cache key’s correctness contract</a>. Keep tenant, representation and permission dimensions wherever they are needed to distinguish answers. Decide separately which bytes determine identity and which determine placement. A tag controls placement; it does not prevent two requests from accidentally using the same full key.</p>
<p>Redis uses CRC16/XMODEM modulo 16,384. You do not normally implement it yourself; use a cluster-aware client and <code>CLUSTER KEYSLOT</code> for diagnosis. The first figure implements that calculation and checks it against native results, including the UTF-8 key <code>café</code>. An empty first brace pair, as in <code>foo&#123;&#125;&#123;bar&#125;</code>, causes the whole key to be hashed rather than skipping to the later pair.</p>

<h2>Cluster clients and MOVED redirects</h2>
<p>A cluster-aware client discovers the topology and maintains a slot-to-node map. It can then send a request directly to the appropriate primary. In this Redis Cluster architecture, contacting the wrong node normally produces a redirect rather than making that node proxy the request.</p>
<p><code>MOVED 8000 host:port</code> says that the contacted node considers another endpoint responsible for slot 8,000. The client should update its routing knowledge and retry appropriately. Our lab first sends a direct GET to the wrong primary and observes MOVED, then retrieves the same value through node-redis’s cluster client.</p>
<p>Bootstrap addresses are therefore only the entrance. The application must also reach the endpoints returned by discovery. A client that can reach one front door but cannot reach advertised shard addresses will fail after discovery or redirection. Check network routes, announced addresses, authentication and TLS for the actual connections made to every node.</p>
<p>With node-redis, <code>createCluster</code> accepts root nodes for discovery and <code>defaults</code> for the connections it creates to discovered nodes. Do not assume settings attached only to one bootstrap connection automatically apply everywhere. The downloadable lab uses three local root addresses and explicitly configures the node connections.</p>
<p>Topology changes also exercise reconnection policy. In an initial lab run, the server replica promoted successfully, but the client’s reads kept failing because reconnection had been disabled and its routing remained stale. With bounded reconnect attempts enabled, the pinned client refreshed its topology and read 15 from the promoted primary. “The cluster failed over” and “this application recovered” are different observations.</p>
<p>Connection retries, command redirects and application retries all consume time. Keep a total request deadline and bounded work, as in <a href="/learn/databases/caching/clients-and-latency/">Redis clients, latency and pipelining</a>. Cluster support does not make a request immune to unavailable nodes, redirect loops or exhausted budgets.</p>

<h2>Resharding, ASK and TRYAGAIN</h2>
<p><strong>Resharding</strong> changes which primary owns a set of slots and transfers their keys. Adding a node creates somewhere to put work; it does not by itself move existing work there. During a move, the owner recorded in a client’s map and the current location of a particular key may differ.</p>
<p>Follow slot 8,000 from B to C. Two keys share the slot, and the first moves before the second.</p>
<ClusterMigration />
<p>At “Move key a”, B is still the slot owner, but a is already on C. B responds with <strong>ASK</strong>. The client sends <code>ASKING</code> on its connection to C, then retries that command there. This temporary detour does not change the client’s permanent slot map.</p>
<p>Later, ownership changes to C. A client still contacting B receives MOVED, which does justify refreshing that map. ASK and MOVED are not interchangeable spellings for “try somewhere else”: they describe different stages of the change.</p>
<p>The lab observes another boundary while a and b are split. An MGET on B receives <strong>TRYAGAIN</strong>, even though the keys have the same hash tag. Once both keys have moved and ownership is updated, the cluster client’s MGET succeeds. Same-slot placement is a prerequisite for this operation, not a promise that migration can never temporarily disrupt it.</p>
<p>The lab uses Redis 8.2’s classic IMPORTING, MIGRATING, MIGRATE and SETSLOT procedure. It prepares the destination first and completes the destination’s ownership change before the source’s. These are carefully ordered administrative operations, not commands to scatter into a production request handler. Use the supported resharding procedure for the deployed version and validate its behaviour under load.</p>
<p>Migration performs real work: serialising values, transferring bytes and reconstructing objects on the destination. Classic MIGRATE blocks during the transfer, so a large key can cause a noticeable pause even when the overall resharding is described as live. Its timeout concerns idle communication time, not a guaranteed total duration. If an I/O error leaves an ambiguous transfer outcome, inspect the source and destination rather than assuming the key has vanished.</p>
<p>Keep headroom on both sides and watch application latency, replication progress and memory while moving data. Resharding may be the remedy for a crowded primary, but the act of moving its contents can temporarily make that primary busier.</p>

<h2>Hot keys and workload skew</h2>
<p>Suppose three primaries have equal numbers of slots. That tells us little about their load. Some slots may hold larger values, some commands may do more work, and one key may receive most of the requests.</p>
<p>A <strong>hot key</strong> concentrates work because one key belongs to one slot and one serving primary. The next model has enough total capacity for its traffic, yet one primary is overloaded. Double the number of primaries and inspect that same primary.</p>
<HotKeyLoad />
<p>With half of 240 requests per second directed at one key, that key contributes 120 units of work to A. Three primaries divide the other 120 units into 40 each, so A receives 160. Six primaries reduce A’s share of the other work to 20, but A still receives 140 against a budget of 100.</p>
<p>Adding shards helps the distributed part of this workload. Moving the hot slot can place it on a quieter primary. Neither divides the hot key itself. A large hash has the same placement problem: its fields are not independently sharded just because they represent many logical records.</p>
<p>Measure concentration in useful units. Request counts help identify frequency, but response bytes, execution time, writes, memory and client decoding can reveal different bottlenecks. A small frequently read string and a repeatedly fetched enormous hash can have the same request rate with very different costs.</p>
<p>For read-heavy hot data, first ask whether each request needs all that work. A smaller projection, avoiding repeated decoding, or coalescing duplicate loads may help. A local cache or reads from replicas can spread some reads, while adding coherence or staleness decisions. They do not distribute writes to one key. We will examine local copies in the next article.</p>
<p>If the data can be partitioned, multiple independent keys can distribute it—but that is an application redesign. A split counter needs an aggregation rule; a split object may lose one-command atomicity. Check the required semantics before choosing a partitioning trick merely because it produces a flatter chart.</p>
<p>Memory is also local to the shard. One primary can evict heavily while another has spare capacity. Summed free memory does not automatically help a key on the full primary. Review per-shard memory pressure and useful hit rates alongside the whole-cluster totals, and include the <a href="/learn/databases/caching/memory-and-performance/">temporary headroom required by operations</a>.</p>

<h2>Cluster availability and replica placement</h2>
<p>Redis Cluster coordinates its own failure detection and replica promotion; Sentinel is not the failover coordinator for this topology. It still uses asynchronous replication, so the <a href="/learn/databases/caching/replication-and-failover/">acknowledged-write and stale-invalidation risks</a> remain.</p>
<p>Availability depends on which nodes survive together. A replica can contain the necessary data yet be unable to obtain the primary votes required for automatic promotion. A surviving primary can hold the requested key yet refuse normal queries because the cluster requires full slot coverage.</p>
<ClusterAvailability />
<p>Fail A alone. B and C form a majority, and A’s replica is available. Promoting that replica restores a serving primary for every shard. Fail A and its replica instead: the other primaries can still agree, but there is no surviving copy of A’s shard in this model.</p>
<p>With the usual <code>cluster-require-full-coverage yes</code>, an uncovered slot range makes the cluster unavailable for normal queries after detection. Setting it to <code>no</code> can let covered portions continue serving while a majority is reachable. It does not restore the missing portion. Nor does it give a minority the authority to promote replicas or accept writes.</p>
<p>There is a separate <code>cluster-allow-reads-when-down</code> setting that can allow local reads in circumstances this figure deliberately excludes. Treat such changes as consistency and availability choices, with explicit application behaviour, rather than generic availability improvements.</p>
<p>Place a primary and its replicas so the intended fault does not remove them together. Also examine how many voting primaries each failure domain contains. Six processes are not six independent failure domains, and a replica count alone does not prove survival of a zone or network partition.</p>
<p>The native lab creates three primaries and one replica for each on one development machine. It kills A after WAIT confirms its test value reached a replica, then observes promotion and a successful cluster-client read. This checks a process failure and the configured client path. It does not measure production failover time or establish that every earlier write survives every fault.</p>

<h2>Decide whether the system needs a cluster</h2>
<p>A cluster is useful when a single primary’s practical memory or execution capacity is insufficient and the application’s keys and work can be divided usefully. It also adds topology-aware clients, more connections, multi-key constraints and more operational states. Establish the bottleneck before making that trade.</p>
<p>Before a move, inventory multi-key commands and scripts, find the largest and hottest keys, and check whether proposed tags create large concentration points. Test the client from the application network through a slot move and a primary failure. Verify the deadline, fallback and stale-answer behaviour the user actually experiences.</p>
<p>The <a href="/learn/databases/caching/examples/redis-cluster-lab.zip" download>downloadable Cluster lab</a> reproduces the slot calculations, CROSSSLOT, MOVED, ASK, TRYAGAIN, migration and promotion described here. It uses Redis 8.2.2, Node 24+ and node-redis 6.3.0, with six owned local processes and automatic cleanup. Its README records the short lab timers, connection policy and limits. Redis Cluster uses database zero; separate logical database numbers are not a substitute for a sound key namespace.</p>
<p>Distribution gives us more places to put independent work. It does not eliminate concentrated work or the cost of keeping copies correct. Next, <a href="/learn/databases/caching/#collection">client-side caching and cache hierarchies</a> follows what happens when each application process keeps another copy of its own.</p>

